{"externally_confirmed_facts":["`javax.net.ssl.SSLContext` represents a secure-socket protocol implementation and is a factory for secure socket factories and `SSLEngine` instances. Its documented initialization event is `SSLContext.init(KeyManager[] km, TrustManager[] tm, SecureRandom random)`. The reported finding refers to the second argument, `tm`: the `TrustManager[]` that supplies sources of peer-authentication trust decisions. The API also documents that, within either manager array, only the first manager of a particular implementation type is used. Source: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/SSLContext.html","The detector wording about an application-defined manager not being observed from a `TrustManagerFactory` is a provenance statement about the value supplied as `tm`; it is not an `SSLContext` API prohibition on application-defined trust-manager classes. The `TrustManager` API expressly states that trust managers may be created by a `TrustManagerFactory` or by implementing a `TrustManager` subtype. Source: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/TrustManager.html","The `SSLContext.init` contract permits `km` and `tm` to be `null`. For a null manager array, installed security providers are searched for the highest-priority appropriate factory implementation; a null `SecureRandom` uses the default implementation. This is API-valid provider-selected behavior, but it is distinct from the CrySL rule's provenance predicates. Source: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/SSLContext.html","Official JSSE documentation supports both factory-produced and custom trust management. It documents obtaining a trust manager from a `TrustManagerFactory` or writing one directly, including by implementing `X509TrustManager`. Where supplied `X509TrustManager` behavior is unsuitable, it documents registering a custom `TrustManagerFactory` or implementing `X509TrustManager` directly and assigning that manager to an `SSLContext` using `init`. Sources: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/security/java-secure-socket-extension-jsse-reference-guide.html and https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/TrustManagerFactory.html","For an `X509TrustManager`, `checkServerTrusted` is documented to build a certification path to a trusted root and return only when the chain is validated and trusted for server SSL authentication; it throws `CertificateException` when the chain is not trusted. The JSSE guide additionally documents checking peer credentials before sending application data and using endpoint identification/verification during the handshake for raw `SSLSocket` or `SSLEngine` usage. A nonempty endpoint-identification algorithm set through `SSLParameters.setEndpointIdentificationAlgorithm` causes verification during the TLS/DTLS handshake. Sources: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/X509TrustManager.html, https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/SSLParameters.html, and https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/security/java-secure-socket-extension-jsse-reference-guide.html","The official sources reviewed do not prescribe a universal remediation requiring a directly implemented trust manager to be replaced with a `TrustManagerFactory` result. They document both approaches. Consequently, the sources do not support weakening certificate validation, trust decisions, or endpoint/hostname verification merely to alter manager provenance.","The reviewed `SSLContext` and JSSE sources define no persisted representation of an `SSLContext` or `TrustManager[]`, and provide no persisted-format migration procedure for changing the way a trust manager is supplied to `SSLContext.init`. `TrustManagerFactory` can be initialized with a `KeyStore` or provider-specific `ManagerFactoryParameters`, but that is not documented as a migration format for this change. Sources: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/TrustManagerFactory.html and https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/SSLContext.html","The reviewed sources do not specify that changing from a directly implemented trust manager to a factory-produced manager changes a TLS wire format. Trust managers make peer-trust decisions, whereas allowed protocols and related connection configuration are exposed through `SSLParameters`. The requested argument `"TLS"` to `SSLContext.getInstance` alone does not document an enabled or negotiated TLS version; `SSLParameters` separately exposes allowed protocols. Sources: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/SSLContext.html and https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/SSLParameters.html","For platform support, the Java SE 25 `SSLContext` API documents mandatory support for `TLSv1.2` and `TLSv1.3` by every implementation and directs readers to implementation release documentation for other supported protocols. `SSLContext`, `TrustManager`, and `X509TrustManager` are documented since Java 1.4; `X509ExtendedTrustManager` and endpoint-identification support are documented since Java 7. The reviewed official documentation provides no migration path specifically for replacing a direct trust manager with a factory-produced one. Sources: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/SSLContext.html, https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/X509TrustManager.html, and https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/X509ExtendedTrustManager.html","The supplied primary CrySL source declares one governed API type: `SPEC javax.net.ssl.SSLContext`. The matching-related portion is, verbatim:
```
SPEC javax.net.ssl.SSLContext

OBJECTS
	java.lang.String protocol;
	javax.net.ssl.KeyManager[] km;
	javax.net.ssl.TrustManager[] tm;
	javax.net.ssl.SSLEngine eng;
	java.security.SecureRandom random;

FORBIDDEN
	getDefault() => Get;

EVENTS
	g1: getInstance(protocol);
	g2: getInstance(protocol, _);
	Get := g1 | g2;

	i1: init(km, tm, random);
	Init := i1;

	se1: eng = createSSLEngine();
	se2: eng = createSSLEngine(_, _);
	Engine := se1 | se2;

ORDER
	Get, Init, Engine?
CONSTRAINTS
	protocol in {"TLSv1.2", "TLSv1.3"};

REQUIRES
	generatedKeyManagers[km];
	generatedTrustManagers[tm];
	randomized[random];
```
Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/SSLContext.crysl","Thus, in the rule text, the declared calls have constrained parameter lists: `getInstance(protocol)` or `getInstance(protocol, _)`; `init(km, tm, random)`; `createSSLEngine()` or `createSSLEngine(_, _)`; and forbidden zero-argument `getDefault()`. The declared object types make `protocol` a `java.lang.String`, `km` a `javax.net.ssl.KeyManager[]`, `tm` a `javax.net.ssl.TrustManager[]`, `eng` a `javax.net.ssl.SSLEngine`, and `random` a `java.security.SecureRandom`. The rule orders `Get, Init, Engine?`, constrains `protocol` to `{"TLSv1.2", "TLSv1.3"}`, and requires `generatedKeyManagers[km]`, `generatedTrustManagers[tm]`, and `randomized[random]`. Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/SSLContext.crysl","No: based on the source text, this is not a name-only rule applicable to arbitrary methods sharing names such as `init`, `getInstance`, `createSSLEngine`, or `getDefault`. Its declared specification scope is `SPEC javax.net.ssl.SSLContext`; the source does not declare a matcher over arbitrary receiver types. Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/SSLContext.crysl","The raw CrySL source does not mention `TrustManagerFactory`, application-defined classes, or the producer semantics of `generatedTrustManagers[tm]`. It declares `generatedTrustManagers[tm]` only in `REQUIRES`. Therefore, that source alone does not state that a trust manager must literally originate from a `TrustManagerFactory`, and it does not define the detector presentation label `UnsatisfiedConstraint`. Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/SSLContext.crysl"],"sources":[{"relevance":"Official Java SE 25 API specification for what `SSLContext` represents, `init` parameter semantics, null arguments, and mandatory protocol support.","limitations":"Documents API contracts, not a particular application's configured managers, provider behavior, enabled protocols, or negotiated protocol.","claim":"`SSLContext` is a secure-socket protocol implementation/factory and `init` accepts key managers, trust managers, and a secure-random source.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/SSLContext.html"},{"relevance":"Official Java SE API for whether a trust manager may be implemented directly.","limitations":"Does not define CogniCrypt's provenance predicate.","claim":"Trust managers may be created through a factory or by implementing a `TrustManager` subtype.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/TrustManager.html"},{"relevance":"Official JSSE guidance on factory-created and direct custom trust managers, peer checks, endpoint identification, and TLS-version compatibility topics.","limitations":"Does not mandate a migration from a direct manager to a factory result and does not describe a particular application's behavior.","claim":"Both custom and factory-based trust-management approaches are documented.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/security/java-secure-socket-extension-jsse-reference-guide.html"},{"relevance":"Official API for provider-based trust-manager creation and trust-material initialization.","limitations":"Does not say that all valid managers must originate from a factory or prescribe a format migration.","claim":"`TrustManagerFactory` provides trust managers based on a `KeyStore` or provider-specific parameters.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/TrustManagerFactory.html"},{"relevance":"Official API defining the documented validation responsibility of `X509TrustManager`.","limitations":"It defines the interface contract; it does not establish what any particular implementation does.","claim":"`checkServerTrusted` must validate a trusted certification path or throw `CertificateException`.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/X509TrustManager.html"},{"relevance":"Primary source for the exact CrySL declaration, events, ordering, literal protocol constraint, and `REQUIRES` predicates.","limitations":"Does not define how `generatedTrustManagers` is produced, detector diagnostics, or application runtime behavior.","claim":"The rule is scoped as `SPEC javax.net.ssl.SSLContext` and requires `generatedTrustManagers[tm]`.","authority":"primary","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/SSLContext.crysl"}],"queries_performed":["Reviewed the supplied official Java SE 25 `SSLContext`, `TrustManager`, `TrustManagerFactory`, `X509TrustManager`, and `SSLParameters` API-documentation URLs.","Reviewed the supplied official Java Secure Socket Extension (JSSE) Reference Guide URL.","Reviewed the supplied primary `SSLContext.crysl` rule source URL for its verbatim declaration and clauses."],"externally_unresolved_questions":["The raw CrySL source does not define which analysis facts or producer rules establish `generatedTrustManagers[tm]`, including whether and how a `TrustManagerFactory` result is recognized.","The raw CrySL source does not define the detector's `UnsatisfiedConstraint` presentation label or any distinction between unresolved and violated predicates; the detector version, diagnostics, and compiled rule set would be needed for that.","The reviewed sources do not identify the application's actual trust manager, trust material, provider, endpoint-identification configuration, enabled protocol list, or negotiated protocol.","The official sources reviewed do not p